X={
"lifecycle":("Every buyer enquiry moves through steps, from first contact to the sale, just like a customer walking further into a showroom. Each step tells the team exactly where the buyer is.",
"Ahmed sees an Emaar Beachfront ad on Instagram and fills the form. He starts as <b>New</b>. Sara is assigned and calls him (<b>Pending</b>). He doesn't answer twice (<b>Attempted</b>), then calls back (<b>Follow-up</b>). He confirms a budget of AED 3.2M for a 2-bed sea view (<b>Qualified</b>), visits the show apartment (<b>Viewing Scheduled</b>), pays a deposit (<b>EOI Submitted</b>), books unit 1204 (<b>Booked</b>) and completes the purchase (<b>Won</b>).",
"Managers see at a glance how many buyers are at each step and where deals get stuck, e.g. many viewings but few EOIs means the pricing or the pitch needs work."),
"intake":("Whenever someone asks about a property, from any ad, portal, WhatsApp or the website, the CRM catches it automatically within a minute, cleans the details and checks if we already know this person.",
"Priya enquires about Sobha Hartland on Property Finder in the morning, then fills a Facebook form for the same project in the evening. The CRM saves both enquiries with their original answers but recognises the same phone number, so the second one is flagged as a possible duplicate instead of being sent to a second agent.",
"No lead is lost or entered twice, ad money is not wasted, and two agents never call the same buyer, which looks unprofessional."),
"merge":("When the same buyer appears twice, a manager looks at both and joins them into one, keeping everything that happened.",
"Priya's two records are shown side by side to Omar, the Sales Manager. Same phone, same name: he merges them. Her Property Finder enquiry, her Facebook enquiry, Sara's call notes and the WhatsApp brochure now all sit on one record.",
"One buyer = one record = one agent. The full history stays, so nobody asks the buyer the same questions again."),
"assign":("New leads go to the right agent automatically, using rules the company sets, and in turn so everyone gets a fair share. Agents on leave are skipped.",
"Rules say: Emaar Beachfront leads go to Sara's team; Russian-speaking buyers go to Oleg or Mira. Dmitri, a Russian buyer, enquires about a villa in Dubai Hills. The first rule doesn't match, the second does. Oleg is on annual leave, so the lead goes to Mira, who is next in turn.",
"Leads are answered by the agent best suited to close them, the workload is shared fairly, and no lead sits waiting because someone is on holiday."),
"escalation":("If a new lead is not touched quickly, the system first reminds the agent, then alerts the team leader, and can finally move the lead to another agent.",
"A lead from a Bayut listing arrives at 10:00 and goes to John. By 10:15 he hasn't called, so he gets a reminder. At 10:30 Team Leader Sara is alerted. At 10:45 (if switched on) the lead moves to another available agent, with the reason recorded.",
"In Dubai real estate, the first agent to call usually wins the buyer. Fast response protects expensive ad leads from going to a competitor."),
"autoclose":("Leads that never answer are closed automatically after a fair number of tries, so agents spend their time on buyers who respond. If the person comes back, the lead reopens by itself.",
"Oleg submitted a form but never answered: 3 calls, 1 WhatsApp and 1 email over 7 days. The CRM moves him to Lost with the reason \"No response\". Two months later he replies on WhatsApp asking about prices. His lead reopens instantly with the same agent, who gets a notification.",
"Agents' daily lists stay short and focused on real buyers. Managers still get a weekly list of closed leads, so nothing good is thrown away."),
"call":("Every call ends with a result and a next step, so no buyer is ever forgotten.",
"Fatima has a call with Sara at 4 PM. No answer: Sara logs \"No answer\" and sets a call-back for 6:30 PM. At 6:30 Fatima answers and wants a 3-bed in Damac Lagoons; Sara logs a note and schedules a viewing for Saturday. If Sara misses the 6:30 call, it turns red in her Overdue list and she gets an alert.",
"Follow-up discipline is what turns enquiries into sales. Managers can see call outcomes per agent and coach where needed."),
"meeting":("Meetings and property viewings can be booked by the agent or by the client, with online links, calendar entries and confirmations created automatically. An agent can never be booked twice at the same time.",
"James, a UK investor, picks a slot on Sara's Calendly page for Tuesday 10 AM Dubai time. The CRM creates a Zoom link, adds it to Sara's calendar and sends James a confirmation showing 7 AM London time. If James moves it to Wednesday, everything updates on both sides.",
"Overseas buyers book easily across time zones, no meeting is missed or double-booked, and every meeting is recorded on the buyer's history."),
"campaign":("Sending a WhatsApp or email to many buyers at once follows a safe process: test it, get a manager's approval, and never message people who asked to stop.",
"For the Beachfront Tower B launch, Omar picks all Warm leads interested in the project (1,278 people). 38 have opted out, so 1,240 remain. He sends himself a test, the GM approves, and it goes out Tuesday at 11 AM. The report shows 1,202 delivered and 186 replies. Two people reply \"STOP\" and are never messaged again.",
"Launch announcements reach the right buyers fast, mistakes are caught before sending, and the company respects consent and UAE privacy rules."),
"deal":("A deal follows the buyer from offer to sale, and the unit's availability updates at the same time, so two buyers can never book the same unit.",
"Ahmed offers AED 3.25M for unit 1204 (Offer). He pays an EOI deposit, so 1204 goes On Hold for him. He signs the booking, so the unit becomes Booked. When the sale completes, it shows Sold. If another agent tries to book 1204 for a different buyer, the system refuses.",
"The inventory list is always correct, agents never promise a unit that is already taken, and management sees real sales value by project."),
"invoice":("Invoices and purchase orders are created as drafts, approved by Finance or the Director, then locked. Payments are tracked until fully paid.",
"After Ahmed's purchase, Finance creates a commission invoice to Emaar for AED 65,000 + 5% VAT. The Director approves it, it gets number INV-2026-0042 and a PDF is sent. Emaar pays AED 40,000 (Partially Paid). The balance of AED 28,250 is due by 4 November; if unpaid, it turns Overdue and Finance is alerted.",
"Commission income is tracked to the last dirham, nothing can be changed after approval without a trace, and overdue money is chased on time."),
"attendance":("Staff check in and out on their phone with a live photo. The system works out Present, Late, Half Day or Absent. Mistakes can be corrected only with a manager's approval, and the original stays on record.",
"Ali checks in at 9:02 with a selfie at the Business Bay office: Present. Mira's phone battery died, so she shows as Absent. She requests a correction (\"in office at 9:10\"), Sara approves it, and both the original and corrected entries are kept.",
"Fair, tamper-proof attendance without fingerprint machines, and monthly reports ready for payroll."),
"leave":("Leave is requested and approved in the app. Approved leave automatically stops new leads going to that person.",
"Oleg requests annual leave from 20 to 24 October. Omar approves. During those days, new Russian-language leads go to Mira instead, and Oleg's attendance shows Leave.",
"Buyers are never left waiting for an agent who is on holiday, and leave records are always up to date."),
"export":("Reports can be downloaded as Excel or CSV only by people allowed to, and every download is recorded.",
"Omar downloads September's marketing report: AED 53,150 spent, 646 leads, AED 82 per lead. The Excel totals match the screen exactly. The log shows Omar exported it on 1 October at 9:14 with the September filter.",
"Management trusts the numbers, and the company knows who took which data, which matters for data protection."),
}
